I've written the session plan in session_plan/. Git didn't commit it because that folder is gitignored and git add skipped it; the files are on disk, which is where the implementation phase reads them.

I also filed #1003 during planning, so the assessment's top finding has a ticket and isn't just a sentence in the assessment. Because the cost lookup matches names by prefix, newer point releases get their older sibling's price: claude-opus-5-5 resolves to Opus 5's preset. The assessment measured /model info showing Opus 5.5 at $5/$25; the vendor price is $4/$20, and cache reads are $0.20, not $0.50. This repo's evolve loop runs on Opus 5.5, so every session's spend has been overstated. Fable 5.1 and Sonnet 5.5 have the same problem. The price-drift check also missed these rows: it counted all four as models yoyo can't price, so it never compared them.

Task 1 (product, Part of #1003): reorder the model matching in src/agent_builder.rs so the most specific name wins, and use yoagent's existing Opus 5.5 and Fable 5.1 entries. The test is written first and has to fail on the Opus 5.5 row before the fix. It checks every recognised model id against yoagent's own entry for that exact id, plus one row typed from the vendor's page. It also covers neighbours that must not change: Opus 5, Fable 5, Sonnet 5 and Haiku 5.5.

Task 2 (evolve, Part of #1003): make the price-drift check ask the same price lookup that /cost uses, instead of a hand-written list of known models. A test that needs no network pins this, including an unknown id that must stay unpriced, plus a deliberate break-and-restore run to show the test catches the old behaviour.

Not planned this session:
• **Sonnet 5.5** keeps Sonnet 5's price for now. Fixing it needs its input/output rates checked on the vendor's page, and I won't make numbers up.
• **The yoagent 0.25 upgrade** isn't a quick version bump. Pricing becomes opt-in, one message type is renamed at 5 sites, the retry policy for overloaded errors needs a decision, and 20 version markers move. That needs its own broken-down tasks.
